hawk@slytherin.ds.psu.edu (Dr. Richard E. Hawkins) writes:

[1.4f]

> Hmm.  And grepping the source,
> 
> hedwig ttyp1:nethack-1.4f>grep DEAD_KOP *c *h
> makedefs.c:     printf("#define DEAD_KOP                DEAD_KOBOLD\n");
> o_init.c:       strcpy(objects[DEAD_KOP].oc_name, "dead Kop");
> onames.h:#define        DEAD_KOP                DEAD_KOBOLD
> 
> The use in strcpy() is as an index to an array, isn't it?  I don't see
> anything in the source that does that, other than the one in onames.h.
> Doesn't that give the same value to DEAD_KOP as DEAD_KOBOLD?  And does
> that make any sense at all??? most dead things get their own values,
> e.g.
> 
> #define DEAD_KOBOLD     31
> 
> but a handfull don't:
> 
> #define CORPSE          DEAD_HUMAN
> #define DEAD_KOP                DEAD_KOBOLD
> #define DEAD_GIANT_SPIDER       DEAD_GIANT_SCORPION
> #define DEAD_ROCKMOLE           DEAD_GIANT_RAT

This is just because what monsters are present in the game is
dependent on what compile-time options you set; if you have KOPS
defined, 'K' is a Kop, otherwise it's a kobold; similarly for SPIDER,
ROCKMOLE, and KAA. See the README file.

(The CORPSE macro is something else, depending on the fact that "dead
human" is the first of the "dead foo" definitions in object.c in order
to do various bits of range checking.)

> Anyway, is the purpose of the strcpy to put "dead Kop" into the
> component oc_name of element DEADCOP of the array object?

It's to overwrite the default (without a KOPS define) name of "dead
kobold" for that particular object, yes. Why doing so should cause a
bus error, that I can't help you with :-)
 
